1 | devops | Add an empirical verification step to the Settlement mode section of docs/operations/deployment.md, which starts around line 35 and which you must read in full first along with docs/operations/env-vars-web.md line 28. DOCS ONLY: change no code, no workflow, no script. THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES. Which settlement a traveller gets is decided by NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CHAT_CARD_PAYMENT. That is a Next.js public env var, so its value is INLINED INTO THE JAVASCRIPT BUNDLE AT BUILD TIME. Its effective position is therefore a property of the last web deploy, not of the environment. Reading vercel env ls tells you what the NEXT build would use, not what the RUNNING build does. The two can disagree silently for as long as nobody redeploys, and nothing in the system reports the disagreement. With a live Duffel token the consequence of getting this wrong is that Olympus pays for real tickets, so a documentary check is not good enough. WHAT TO ADD: a short numbered procedure, placed inside the existing Settlement mode section rather than as a new top level section, that establishes what the RUNNING production build actually does. Do not invent the method. Work out what is genuinely observable today and write down what you verified, choosing among at least these and saying why you picked what you picked. One, the API already logs alert=standalone_balance_settlement on every booking that resolves to Balance, per docs/operations/env-vars-api.md, so a booking that settles from Balance is countable after the fact in the logs. Two, a card settled booking mints a manual capture Stripe PaymentIntent, so its presence or absence is observable in Stripe. Three, the inlined flag value is literally present in the served JavaScript bundle, so it can in principle be read from the running site, but say honestly how brittle that is before recommending it. THE PROCEDURE MUST BE RUNNABLE BY A PERSON WHO IS NOT YOU, so give real commands with real hostnames from docs/operations, not placeholders, and state the expected output for both the card case and the balance case so the reader can tell which one they are looking at. State plainly what a WRONG result looks like and what to do about it, which is that the flag position and the running bundle disagree and the fix is a web redeploy rather than an env change. Cross reference from the Settlement mode table so a reader who lands on the table is sent to the check. ALSO record the underlying asymmetry once, plainly: docs/operations/env-vars-web.md line 9 already notes the web side has no equivalent of the API bidirectional env lint because Next inlines these at build time and a missing var resolves to undefined rather than a startup error. That is why this manual step exists, and saying so stops someone deleting it as redundant. Do NOT propose or build a CI check for this: that would be a different piece of work and this task is documentation. HOUSE STYLE: do not use the long dash character anywhere in any line you add, in the doc, the commit message or the PR body. Use a comma, a colon, parentheses or a new sentence. Open a PR referencing issue 2659 with Refs and no closing keyword, since that issue is closed and this is follow-on hardening rather than a fix to it. Omit any Co-Authored-By trailer. Do not merge. | docs/settlement-empirical-check

2 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 1 change on branch docs/settlement-empirical-check. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no edits. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: could an operator follow this procedure exactly, get a clean result, and still be wrong about what the running site does. That is the only failure that matters here, because the whole point of the step is to catch a disagreement that no other signal reports. FIRST, check every command in the procedure actually runs. Run them yourself where they are read only and safe to run, and say which ones you ran and what they returned. A runbook step that has never been executed is a guess written in the imperative mood. Flag any placeholder hostname, any invented flag, any command that would need credentials the reader was not told to obtain, and any output the doc claims to expect that you did not observe. SECOND, verify the method genuinely observes the RUNNING build rather than the environment. If any step reads an env var, a dashboard, a config file or another doc, that step does not answer the question the section is asking and should be flagged, no matter how convenient it is. THIRD, confirm the doc states what a WRONG result looks like, not only a right one, and that the stated remedy is a web redeploy rather than an env change, since an env change alone would leave the running bundle untouched and would look like a fix. FOURTH, confirm nothing in the new text contradicts docs/operations/env-vars-web.md line 28 or the Settlement mode table above it, and that the table now points at the check. FIFTH, confirm the diff is docs only, nothing under apps/ or .github/ or scripts/. SIXTH, confirm no long dash character appears in any added line, including the PR body and commit message. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC SETTLEMENT EMPIRICAL CHECK, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE, per-finding severity and file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | docs/settlement-empirical-check
